  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced capacity of constituents and stakeholders to comply with international labour standards and address unacceptable forms of work</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The One ILO contributes to strengthening Ethiopia&#x2019;s capacity to comply with international labour standards through an integrated approach that reinforces labour administration and inspection systems while improving occupational safety and health (OSH) outcomes across priority sectors.

At the institutional level, support is provided for strengthening of labour administration and inspection systems by enhancing technical capacity, improving coordination among relevant authorities, and promoting the use of evidence-based tools and methodologies. This contributes to more effective enforcement of labour legislation and improved compliance with international labour standards.
In parallel, national capacity will be strengthened to realize safe and healthy working environments through the operationalization of OSH frameworks and systems. This includes improved recording and notification systems, strengthened institutional mechanisms for monitoring and reporting, and increased application of preventive approaches at sectoral and workplace levels. These efforts contribute to the progressive institutionalization of OSH systems within national labour governance structures.

The support also lays the foundation for more effective and evidence-based wage-setting mechanisms. Through the generation of updated wage data, institutional analysis, and support to tripartite dialogue, it strengthens the capacity of constituents to engage in informed discussions on wage policy and reform. While policy-level results are expected beyond the current period, these efforts contribute to building the necessary conditions for more transparent and evidence-based wage-setting processes.
Overall, the ILO support promotes a systems-based approach that links institutional strengthening, policy development, and workplace practices. This results in enhanced capacity of constituents and stakeholders to enforce labour standards, improve working conditions, and advance decent work in line with ILS.</narrative>
  </description>
